  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="788e-3f38-973a-bb6f" name="Later Hoplite Greek (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #19 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). After the Peloponnesian War the city states continued to squabble with each other, fuelled by Persian gold. They also provided solid mercenary troops to Persia, Egypt and Carthage and were involved in expeditions to all these areas. The period saw the eclipse of Athens and the rise of Thebes. Many armies were formed from coalitions of several city states under the leadership of one of the main powers. Greece was subjugated by Phillip II of Macedon and remained under heavy Macedonian influence, eventually adopted the Macedonian military system. The main difference from the earlier period is the use of much more effective light troops, and cavalry. Note: Greek armies may not have unit or army standards, but instead use the Oracles rule. 

ARMY COMPOSITIONS (pick one in the Army composition list):
[Later Hoplite Greek - Epirus]
  Cavalry: up to 10%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 25%
  Light Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25% Cavalry: up to 10% May not take elite troops Infantry: At least 25% May take 0-1 elite hoplites, every second unit of regular (non-levy) hoplites may take light armour Light Troops: up to 25% Allies &amp; Mercenaries Allies up to 25% May take illyrians (not slave troops) May take allies from Sparta or Thebes, but not both This kingdom was located in the northwest of Greece and modern albania and was regarded as cultural and ecenomic backwater by the major greek states. It suffered from continual raids by Illyrian tribes but by the early 4th century BC had begun to extend its control over several of these, gaining a measure of Stability. The list covers early Epirus up to the modernisation of the army by Pyrrhus
[Later Hoplite Greek - Minor States]
  Cavalry: up to 10%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 50%
  Light Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25% Cavalry: up to 10% May not take Elite Cavalry. Infantry: At least 50% May take 0-1 Elite Hoplites. Must field at least one unit of Militia Hoplites. Light Troops: up to 25%
[Later Hoplite Greek - Sparta]
  Cavalry: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 50%
  Light Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25% A Spartan King may upgrade to Ld10 (+100). Cavalry: up to 25%. May not take Elite Cavalry. Infantry: At least 50% At least one phalanx must upgrade to Elite. May not take Militia. Light Troops: up to 25% Allies &amp; Mercenaries: up to 25% May chose allies from Minor States in this list. Elite hoplites are Spartiates and there must be at least one unit in the army upgraded to Elite status. Spartiates are WS4, Drilled, Stubborn, and Trained Phalanx costing 23 points a model.
[Later Hoplite Greek - Tegea]
  Cavalry: up to 10%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 25%
  Light Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25% Cavalry: up to 10% May not take elite troops Infantry: At least 25% every third unit may be upgraded to elite Light Troops: up to 25% Allies &amp; Mercenaries Allies up to 25% May take allies from Sparta or Thebes but not both This State was located in the Peloponnese and was regarded by Sparta as lying within its sphere of influence. The states clashed on several occasions and Sparta was not always victorious. Tegea became known for the tough fighting power of its hoplites. While often fighting as a spartan ally. Tegea joined the Arcadian league in the 4th century BC and fought with Thebes against Sparta at the second battle of Mantinea in 326BC
[Later Hoplite Greek - Thebes]
  Cavalry: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: up to 25%, at least 50%
  Characters: up to 25% Cavalry: up to 25% Infantry: At least 50%. Elite and Regular Hoplites cost +2 points receiving a maximum of +3 combat resolution bonus for ranks rather than the usual +2. Light Troops: up to 25% Allies &amp; Mercenaries: up to 25% May chose allies from Minor States in this list. May upgrade Heavy Cavalry and any Hoplites. Elite Theban Hoplites in the Peloponnesian War period cost 20 points a model and can receive an extra rank bonus if they have enough ranks, as well as WS4 Trained Phalanx. In earlier periods they cost 18 points are WS4 Trained Phalanx only. Regular Theban Hoplites in the Peloponnesian War period cost 15 pts and also receive the extra rank bonus as above.
[Later Hoplite Greek - Thessaly]
  Cavalry: at least 25%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 25%
  Light Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: up to 25% Cavalry: at least 25% must include at least one elite cavalry unit. Infantry: At least 25% May not include elite hoplites Light Troops: up to 25% Allies &amp; Mercenaries up to 25% May select from Early Macedonian or Late Hoplite Greek Minor States. All cavalry cost +2 points and are M8. Thessaly was noted for its extensive use of cavalry and the quality of the horses it raised. The army general would be referred to as Tagus; in 344BC Philip II of Macedon was declared Tagus for Life.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
